INSERT INTO sat_questions (question_id, external_id, vault_id, disclosed_item_id, source, program, test_type, test_type_desc, domain, primary_class_cd, skill_cd, skill_desc, difficulty, score_band_range_cd, question_type, stimulus_html, stem_html, answer_options, correct_answer, answer_keys, rationale_html, correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale, image_count, parent_template_name, parent_template_id, template_cluster_id, template_cluster_name, origin, position, answer_style, section, raw_payload, created_date, updated_date) VALUES
    ($SATQ$3882ddf6$SATQ$, $SATQ$e68d49e4-3326-4ad6-86cd-1be7dce09141$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$c3e417e1-b90e-4046-b63f-074ffb96ced7$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inferences$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>To investigate the history of plate subduction&mdash;when one of Earth&rsquo;s tectonic plates slides beneath another&mdash;Sarah M. Aarons and colleagues compared ancient rocks from the Acasta Gneiss Complex in Canada to modern rocks. Using isotope analysis, the researchers found that Acasta rocks dating to about 4.02 billion years ago (bya) most strongly resemble modern rocks formed in a plume setting (an area in which hot rocks from Earth&rsquo;s mantle flow upward into the crust). By contrast, they found that Acasta rocks dating to about 3.75 bya and 3.6 bya have an isotope composition that is similar to that of modern rocks formed in a subduction setting. Aarons&rsquo;s team therefore concluded that <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b9fabb85-82f2-4658-92d4-5caffd955c05","content":"<p>subduction-like processes began occurring in some locations no later than 3.75 bya.</p>"},{"id":"811c4d29-5069-40e3-934a-ebd247b29039","content":"<p>subduction replaced mantle plume formation as the most common geological process by about 4.02 bya.</p>"},{"id":"8efec816-aeca-4b42-b9f5-f9cf6d9f59c4","content":"<p>the majority of the rocks in the Acasta Gneiss Complex formed through subduction.</p>"},{"id":"08e9a46f-abb1-444e-bda1-c326ffbf43a9","content":"<p>the rocks in the Acasta Gneiss Complex are of a more recent origin than scientists previously thought.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["b9fabb85-82f2-4658-92d4-5caffd955c05"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. Because researchers found &ldquo;Acasta rocks dating to about 3.75 bya&rdquo; were similar to &ldquo;modern rocks formed in a subduction setting,&rdquo; we can infer that subduction-like processes must have begun occurring in the Acasta Gneiss Complex by this time. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. We only know about geological processes at the Acasta Gneiss Complex, so we do not have information to make inferences about geological processes in general. Also, notice that the rocks dated to 4.02 bya were found to still be formed in a plume setting, so the transition must have happened after this time. Choice C is incorrect. There&rsquo;s no mention in the passage of what proportion of the rocks within Acasta Gneiss Complex were formed through subduction, so there&rsquo;s no basis for this inference. Choice D is incorrect. The passage discusses various rocks in the Acasta Gneiss Complex that are dated to different periods of time, but nothing in the passage suggests that these dates are or were wrong. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-INF-M010$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$abe53c46-aae7-447a-81d0-1c94c21d6b39$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"3882ddf6","external_id":"e68d49e4-3326-4ad6-86cd-1be7dce09141","vaultid":"c3e417e1-b90e-4046-b63f-074ffb96ced7","uId":"6b0687a6-0d4c-42d8-be10-9807671149e8","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"INF","skill_desc":"Inferences","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>To investigate the history of plate subduction&mdash;when one of Earth&rsquo;s tectonic plates slides beneath another&mdash;Sarah M. Aarons and colleagues compared ancient rocks from the Acasta Gneiss Complex in Canada to modern rocks. Using isotope analysis, the researchers found that Acasta rocks dating to about 4.02 billion years ago (bya) most strongly resemble modern rocks formed in a plume setting (an area in which hot rocks from Earth&rsquo;s mantle flow upward into the crust). By contrast, they found that Acasta rocks dating to about 3.75 bya and 3.6 bya have an isotope composition that is similar to that of modern rocks formed in a subduction setting. Aarons&rsquo;s team therefore concluded that <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b9fabb85-82f2-4658-92d4-5caffd955c05","content":"<p>subduction-like processes began occurring in some locations no later than 3.75 bya.</p>"},{"id":"811c4d29-5069-40e3-934a-ebd247b29039","content":"<p>subduction replaced mantle plume formation as the most common geological process by about 4.02 bya.</p>"},{"id":"8efec816-aeca-4b42-b9f5-f9cf6d9f59c4","content":"<p>the majority of the rocks in the Acasta Gneiss Complex formed through subduction.</p>"},{"id":"08e9a46f-abb1-444e-bda1-c326ffbf43a9","content":"<p>the rocks in the Acasta Gneiss Complex are of a more recent origin than scientists previously thought.</p>"}],"keys":["b9fabb85-82f2-4658-92d4-5caffd955c05"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. Because researchers found &ldquo;Acasta rocks dating to about 3.75 bya&rdquo; were similar to &ldquo;modern rocks formed in a subduction setting,&rdquo; we can infer that subduction-like processes must have begun occurring in the Acasta Gneiss Complex by this time. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. We only know about geological processes at the Acasta Gneiss Complex, so we do not have information to make inferences about geological processes in general. Also, notice that the rocks dated to 4.02 bya were found to still be formed in a plume setting, so the transition must have happened after this time. Choice C is incorrect. There&rsquo;s no mention in the passage of what proportion of the rocks within Acasta Gneiss Complex were formed through subduction, so there&rsquo;s no basis for this inference. Choice D is incorrect. The passage discusses various rocks in the Acasta Gneiss Complex that are dated to different periods of time, but nothing in the passage suggests that these dates are or were wrong. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-INF-M010","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"abe53c46-aae7-447a-81d0-1c94c21d6b39","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959368,"updateDate":1691007959368}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959368, 1691007959368),
    ($SATQ$38d75269$SATQ$, $SATQ$0609b216-e92c-4fcd-8833-8f8738ce30d0$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Janet Echelman is a sculptor and fiber artist. She has installed giant sculptures all over the world. Echelman uses bright and flowing materials, which mimic the wind. <span style="text-decoration: underline;" role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content">However, while her sculptures appear as delicate as a breeze, they are actually very durable.</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which quotation from an article about Echelman&rsquo;s sculptures, if true, would most effectively illustrate the underlined claim?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"e07b7d63-af49-49e5-9717-5464ee30ffb2","content":"<p>&ldquo;Echelman uses a special program that makes a 3D model of the sculpture.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"b79f33b7-bc3e-4a95-a7d4-a8a6a0c16f9c","content":"<p>&ldquo;The first part of planning a new sculpture is done using paper and pencil, and then a digital program is used to finalize the design.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"dd41e176-b1ce-48c4-abc3-66d3c3eca7da","content":"<p>&ldquo;The materials that Echelman uses to build her sculptures are both flexible and strong.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"fa2871d6-b327-4f15-bbb5-0aed2e07435e","content":"<p>&ldquo;Each sculpture is designed to reflect local landmarks from the area in which it is eventually installed.&rdquo;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["dd41e176-b1ce-48c4-abc3-66d3c3eca7da"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it presents the quotation that most effectively illustrates the claim that Echelman&rsquo;s sculptures appear delicate but are in fact quite durable. The text explains that Echelman&rsquo;s sculptures include flowing shapes that mimic the wind. If it is true that the materials she uses are both flexible and strong, that would help explain why the works are durable even though they appear delicate.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the claim in the text is not about how Echelman models her work before sculpting. Choice B is incorrect because the claim in the text is not about the planning and design phases of Echelman&rsquo;s work. Choice D is incorrect because the claim in the text is not about how the sculptures relate to their locations.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"38d75269","external_id":"0609b216-e92c-4fcd-8833-8f8738ce30d0","vaultid":null,"uId":"87dd362e-4894-4efc-882e-b414805d2c9b","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Janet Echelman is a sculptor and fiber artist. She has installed giant sculptures all over the world. Echelman uses bright and flowing materials, which mimic the wind. <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\">However, while her sculptures appear as delicate as a breeze, they are actually very durable.</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which quotation from an article about Echelman&rsquo;s sculptures, if true, would most effectively illustrate the underlined claim?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"e07b7d63-af49-49e5-9717-5464ee30ffb2","content":"<p>&ldquo;Echelman uses a special program that makes a 3D model of the sculpture.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"b79f33b7-bc3e-4a95-a7d4-a8a6a0c16f9c","content":"<p>&ldquo;The first part of planning a new sculpture is done using paper and pencil, and then a digital program is used to finalize the design.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"dd41e176-b1ce-48c4-abc3-66d3c3eca7da","content":"<p>&ldquo;The materials that Echelman uses to build her sculptures are both flexible and strong.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"fa2871d6-b327-4f15-bbb5-0aed2e07435e","content":"<p>&ldquo;Each sculpture is designed to reflect local landmarks from the area in which it is eventually installed.&rdquo;</p>"}],"keys":["dd41e176-b1ce-48c4-abc3-66d3c3eca7da"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it presents the quotation that most effectively illustrates the claim that Echelman&rsquo;s sculptures appear delicate but are in fact quite durable. The text explains that Echelman&rsquo;s sculptures include flowing shapes that mimic the wind. If it is true that the materials she uses are both flexible and strong, that would help explain why the works are durable even though they appear delicate.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the claim in the text is not about how Echelman models her work before sculpting. Choice B is incorrect because the claim in the text is not about the planning and design phases of Echelman&rsquo;s work. Choice D is incorrect because the claim in the text is not about how the sculptures relate to their locations.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669203,"updateDate":1743430669203}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669203, 1743430669203),
    ($SATQ$38e79659$SATQ$, $SATQ$39859646-9f3d-440b-9296-afa336dc7198$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$45cc3817-7601-42d4-b23b-99d1009f2823$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><figure class="table"><table class="gdr"><caption style="caption-side: top;"><p style="text-align: center;">Attendance and Cost of Hosting for Past Four US World&rsquo;s Fairs</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope="col" style="text-align: center; vertical-align: bottom;">World&rsquo;s fairs held in the US</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center; vertical-align: bottom;">Cost (in US dollars)</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center; vertical-align: bottom;">Number of visitors</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Century 21 Exposition (1962)</th><td>$47 million</td><td>9.60 million</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">HemisFair &rsquo;68</th><td>$156 million</td><td>6.40 million</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">1984 World&rsquo;s Fair</th><td>$350 million</td><td>7.35 million</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Expo &rsquo;74</th><td>$78 million</td><td>5.60 million</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>
<p>Huge international exhibitions known as world&rsquo;s fairs have been held since 1851, but the United States hasn&rsquo;t hosted one since 1984. Architecture expert Mina Chow argues that this is because some people think the events are too expensive and not popular enough. For example, the 1984 World&rsquo;s Fair cost $350 million and had only <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the example?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"2e965ed0-b2b8-4d67-a6c4-6adea216590f","content":"<p>7.35 million visitors.</p>"},{"id":"266c8b74-46b5-43e4-9baf-01831690110e","content":"<p>9.60 million visitors.</p>"},{"id":"c86d7cdf-4b0c-4036-94de-ea0f2ee8df6c","content":"<p>6.40 million visitors.</p>"},{"id":"71ac4e2d-9ce5-435b-959f-5f2b8d9b2156","content":"<p>5.60 million visitors.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["2e965ed0-b2b8-4d67-a6c4-6adea216590f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most effectively uses data from the table to complete the example of the high cost and low popularity of world&rsquo;s fairs. The text presents Chow&rsquo;s argument that the United States hasn&rsquo;t hosted a world&rsquo;s fair since 1984 because people think these exhibitions are overly expensive and insufficiently popular. The text then cites the 1984 World&rsquo;s Fair as an example, noting that it cost $350 million. Since the example should illustrate both high cost and insufficient popularity, the best completion of the example is the information from the table that the 1984 World&rsquo;s Fair had 7.35 million visitors. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it misrepresents data from the table. The table indicates that the 1984 World&rsquo;s Fair, which is the world&rsquo;s fair used as an example in the text, had 7.35 million, not 9.60 million, visitors.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because it misrepresents data from the table. The table indicates that the 1984 World&rsquo;s Fair, which is the world&rsquo;s fair used as an example in the text, had 7.35 million, not 6.40 million, visitors.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because it misrepresents data from the table. The table indicates that the 1984 World&rsquo;s Fair, which is the world&rsquo;s fair used as an example in the text, had 7.35 million, not 5.60 million, visitors.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-544-QUA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$a320453e-b49d-465b-a0c7-bc4d50550a54$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"38e79659","external_id":"39859646-9f3d-440b-9296-afa336dc7198","vaultid":"45cc3817-7601-42d4-b23b-99d1009f2823","uId":"74988497-ce9e-4062-8422-fa6144ded908","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><figure class=\"table\"><table class=\"gdr\"><caption style=\"caption-side: top;\"><p style=\"text-align: center;\">Attendance and Cost of Hosting for Past Four US World&rsquo;s Fairs</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center; vertical-align: bottom;\">World&rsquo;s fairs held in the US</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center; vertical-align: bottom;\">Cost (in US dollars)</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center; vertical-align: bottom;\">Number of visitors</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Century 21 Exposition (1962)</th><td>$47 million</td><td>9.60 million</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">HemisFair &rsquo;68</th><td>$156 million</td><td>6.40 million</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">1984 World&rsquo;s Fair</th><td>$350 million</td><td>7.35 million</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Expo &rsquo;74</th><td>$78 million</td><td>5.60 million</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>\n<p>Huge international exhibitions known as world&rsquo;s fairs have been held since 1851, but the United States hasn&rsquo;t hosted one since 1984. Architecture expert Mina Chow argues that this is because some people think the events are too expensive and not popular enough. For example, the 1984 World&rsquo;s Fair cost $350 million and had only <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the example?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"2e965ed0-b2b8-4d67-a6c4-6adea216590f","content":"<p>7.35 million visitors.</p>"},{"id":"266c8b74-46b5-43e4-9baf-01831690110e","content":"<p>9.60 million visitors.</p>"},{"id":"c86d7cdf-4b0c-4036-94de-ea0f2ee8df6c","content":"<p>6.40 million visitors.</p>"},{"id":"71ac4e2d-9ce5-435b-959f-5f2b8d9b2156","content":"<p>5.60 million visitors.</p>"}],"keys":["2e965ed0-b2b8-4d67-a6c4-6adea216590f"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most effectively uses data from the table to complete the example of the high cost and low popularity of world&rsquo;s fairs. The text presents Chow&rsquo;s argument that the United States hasn&rsquo;t hosted a world&rsquo;s fair since 1984 because people think these exhibitions are overly expensive and insufficiently popular. The text then cites the 1984 World&rsquo;s Fair as an example, noting that it cost $350 million. Since the example should illustrate both high cost and insufficient popularity, the best completion of the example is the information from the table that the 1984 World&rsquo;s Fair had 7.35 million visitors. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it misrepresents data from the table. The table indicates that the 1984 World&rsquo;s Fair, which is the world&rsquo;s fair used as an example in the text, had 7.35 million, not 9.60 million, visitors.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because it misrepresents data from the table. The table indicates that the 1984 World&rsquo;s Fair, which is the world&rsquo;s fair used as an example in the text, had 7.35 million, not 6.40 million, visitors.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because it misrepresents data from the table. The table indicates that the 1984 World&rsquo;s Fair, which is the world&rsquo;s fair used as an example in the text, had 7.35 million, not 5.60 million, visitors.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-544-QUA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"a320453e-b49d-465b-a0c7-bc4d50550a54","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959338,"updateDate":1691007959338}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959338, 1691007959338),
    ($SATQ$38facbad$SATQ$, $SATQ$0d431820-b3fc-41fe-9b35-b26b78b6f0ad$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inferences$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Studies conducted in individual Western societies have demonstrated associations between personality traits and five musical factors (mellowness, unpretentiousness, sophistication, intensity, and contemporariness) underlying individual preferences for styles of Western music. To investigate these associations across cultures, David <span data-ssml-sub-alias="M">M.</span> Greenberg <span data-ssml-sub-alias="et al">et al.</span> collected music-preference assessments for Western genres and self-reported personality traits from participants in fifty-three countries across six continents. The study confirmed that the five-factor framework accurately captured participants&rsquo; tastes in Western music, and, moreover, the study found similar correlations between patterns of these factors and of personality traits, suggesting that <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d4b86fd0-5b1f-41b1-b11f-ad38ef009252","content":"<p>across cultural contexts, people who share similar profiles of personality traits tend to prefer listening to similar types of Western music.</p>"},{"id":"90fa60c8-8b21-4e40-8e07-41f58384a887","content":"<p>the five-factor framework can likely be used to predict preferences for non-Western music styles based on personality traits even if the characteristics of those styles substantially differ from characteristics of Western music styles.</p>"},{"id":"a97bad9c-e2fd-49a0-ac45-250b7ab6b987","content":"<p>the strength of the relationship between personality traits and musical preferences varies less across cultures than researchers had previously assumed.</p>"},{"id":"816b1f2a-0d14-4148-b398-cc0261f079a4","content":"<p>people with a relatively high degree of familiarity with Western music styles are likely to express stronger preferences for those styles than people with a relatively low degree of familiarity with those styles are.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["d4b86fd0-5b1f-41b1-b11f-ad38ef009252"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the cross-cultural study on personality traits and music preferences. The text first explains that previous studies in individual Western societies had found links between personality traits and five musical factors that shape individual preferences for Western music styles. This finding suggests that people in those societies who have similar personality traits likely prefer listening to similar types of Western music. The text goes on to state that Greenberg et al. conducted a study that expanded this research to a broader, cross-cultural context (fifty-three countries across six continents). This study validated that the five-factor framework accurately represented participants&rsquo; preferences for Western music and that the study found patterns linking those preferences to personality traits. It follows, then, that people with similar profiles of personality traits tend to prefer similar types of Western music across cultural contexts.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because Greenberg et al.&rsquo;s study examined only preferences for Western music styles, so the text provides no basis for inferring that the five-factor framework could predict preferences for non-Western music styles. The study was specifically limited to Western musical styles, and extending the study&rsquo;s findings to non-Western music would go beyond what the study investigated or supports. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t mention what researchers had previously assumed about cultural variation in the strength of the relationship between personality traits and musical preferences. The text presents the cross-cultural consistency of the association between musical preferences and personality traits as a new finding but doesn&rsquo;t mention previous expectations or assumptions about this association. Choice D is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t address familiarity with Western music styles or suggest that familiarity influences preference strength for those styles. Greenberg et al.&rsquo;s study focused on the relationship between personality traits and musical preferences across cultural contexts, not on how exposure or familiarity might affect those preferences.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"38facbad","external_id":"0d431820-b3fc-41fe-9b35-b26b78b6f0ad","vaultid":null,"uId":"d97b64ce-c83b-4872-bf45-a6fd50ca54e7","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"INF","skill_desc":"Inferences","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Studies conducted in individual Western societies have demonstrated associations between personality traits and five musical factors (mellowness, unpretentiousness, sophistication, intensity, and contemporariness) underlying individual preferences for styles of Western music. To investigate these associations across cultures, David <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"M\">M.</span> Greenberg <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"et al\">et al.</span> collected music-preference assessments for Western genres and self-reported personality traits from participants in fifty-three countries across six continents. The study confirmed that the five-factor framework accurately captured participants&rsquo; tastes in Western music, and, moreover, the study found similar correlations between patterns of these factors and of personality traits, suggesting that <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d4b86fd0-5b1f-41b1-b11f-ad38ef009252","content":"<p>across cultural contexts, people who share similar profiles of personality traits tend to prefer listening to similar types of Western music.</p>"},{"id":"90fa60c8-8b21-4e40-8e07-41f58384a887","content":"<p>the five-factor framework can likely be used to predict preferences for non-Western music styles based on personality traits even if the characteristics of those styles substantially differ from characteristics of Western music styles.</p>"},{"id":"a97bad9c-e2fd-49a0-ac45-250b7ab6b987","content":"<p>the strength of the relationship between personality traits and musical preferences varies less across cultures than researchers had previously assumed.</p>"},{"id":"816b1f2a-0d14-4148-b398-cc0261f079a4","content":"<p>people with a relatively high degree of familiarity with Western music styles are likely to express stronger preferences for those styles than people with a relatively low degree of familiarity with those styles are.</p>"}],"keys":["d4b86fd0-5b1f-41b1-b11f-ad38ef009252"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the cross-cultural study on personality traits and music preferences. The text first explains that previous studies in individual Western societies had found links between personality traits and five musical factors that shape individual preferences for Western music styles. This finding suggests that people in those societies who have similar personality traits likely prefer listening to similar types of Western music. The text goes on to state that Greenberg et al. conducted a study that expanded this research to a broader, cross-cultural context (fifty-three countries across six continents). This study validated that the five-factor framework accurately represented participants&rsquo; preferences for Western music and that the study found patterns linking those preferences to personality traits. It follows, then, that people with similar profiles of personality traits tend to prefer similar types of Western music across cultural contexts.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because Greenberg et al.&rsquo;s study examined only preferences for Western music styles, so the text provides no basis for inferring that the five-factor framework could predict preferences for non-Western music styles. The study was specifically limited to Western musical styles, and extending the study&rsquo;s findings to non-Western music would go beyond what the study investigated or supports. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t mention what researchers had previously assumed about cultural variation in the strength of the relationship between personality traits and musical preferences. The text presents the cross-cultural consistency of the association between musical preferences and personality traits as a new finding but doesn&rsquo;t mention previous expectations or assumptions about this association. Choice D is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t address familiarity with Western music styles or suggest that familiarity influences preference strength for those styles. Greenberg et al.&rsquo;s study focused on the relationship between personality traits and musical preferences across cultural contexts, not on how exposure or familiarity might affect those preferences.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771167,"updateDate":1776362771167}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771167, 1776362771167),
    ($SATQ$39de2206$SATQ$, $SATQ$d227c514-23b6-4498-90bf-633ef71950d4$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$bc6fd740-f9d9-4a79-bc2c-2f9b3ea3a060$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><em>The Post Office </em>is a 1912 play by Rabindranath Tagore, originally written in Bengali. The character Amal is a young boy who imagines that the people he sees passing the window of his home are carefree even when engaged in work or chores, as is evident when he says to the daughter of a flower seller, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which quotation from <em>The Post Office </em>most effectively illustrates the claim?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"8630bd76-9ced-4367-bdfb-7e4d11e8fc8d","content":"<p>&ldquo;I see, you don&rsquo;t wish to stop; I don&rsquo;t care to stay on here either.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"d49a55a7-388b-4c87-9236-ff99625ebfa4","content":"<p>&ldquo;Oh, flower gathering? That is why your feet seem so glad and your anklets jingle so merrily as you walk.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"8ad4be81-4046-4016-a21f-d1e452f04607","content":"<p>&ldquo;I&rsquo;ll pay when I grow up&mdash;before I leave to look for work out on the other side of that stream there.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"fd903b57-7bc6-43ce-8449-92e655a7359d","content":"<p>&ldquo;Wish I could be out too. Then I would pick some flowers for you from the very topmost branches right out of sight.&rdquo;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["d49a55a7-388b-4c87-9236-ff99625ebfa4"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most effectively illustrates the claim that Amal imagines the people he sees are carefree even when engaged in work. In the quotation, Amal observes that the flower seller&rsquo;s daughter is &ldquo;flower gathering,&rdquo; or working, as the text indicates. Moreover, Amal notes that the daughter&rsquo;s feet &ldquo;seem so glad&rdquo; and her &ldquo;anklets jingle so merrily,&rdquo; suggesting that Amal believes that the flower seller&rsquo;s daughter is cheerful. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the quotation makes no observation about the cheerful mood of the flower seller&rsquo;s daughter. Choice C is incorrect because the quotation discusses how Amal envisions his future, not the feelings of the flower seller&rsquo;s daughter. Choice D is incorrect because the quotation discusses Amal&rsquo;s wishes, not the feelings of the flower seller&rsquo;s daughter. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-402-LIT$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$3e4792a5-9658-42ee-84d7-228912f12c73$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"39de2206","external_id":"d227c514-23b6-4498-90bf-633ef71950d4","vaultid":"bc6fd740-f9d9-4a79-bc2c-2f9b3ea3a060","uId":"92bb63e0-0248-4c7b-9927-27a0f0be36fa","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><em>The Post Office </em>is a 1912 play by Rabindranath Tagore, originally written in Bengali. The character Amal is a young boy who imagines that the people he sees passing the window of his home are carefree even when engaged in work or chores, as is evident when he says to the daughter of a flower seller, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which quotation from <em>The Post Office </em>most effectively illustrates the claim?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"8630bd76-9ced-4367-bdfb-7e4d11e8fc8d","content":"<p>&ldquo;I see, you don&rsquo;t wish to stop; I don&rsquo;t care to stay on here either.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"d49a55a7-388b-4c87-9236-ff99625ebfa4","content":"<p>&ldquo;Oh, flower gathering? That is why your feet seem so glad and your anklets jingle so merrily as you walk.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"8ad4be81-4046-4016-a21f-d1e452f04607","content":"<p>&ldquo;I&rsquo;ll pay when I grow up&mdash;before I leave to look for work out on the other side of that stream there.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"fd903b57-7bc6-43ce-8449-92e655a7359d","content":"<p>&ldquo;Wish I could be out too. Then I would pick some flowers for you from the very topmost branches right out of sight.&rdquo;</p>"}],"keys":["d49a55a7-388b-4c87-9236-ff99625ebfa4"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most effectively illustrates the claim that Amal imagines the people he sees are carefree even when engaged in work. In the quotation, Amal observes that the flower seller&rsquo;s daughter is &ldquo;flower gathering,&rdquo; or working, as the text indicates. Moreover, Amal notes that the daughter&rsquo;s feet &ldquo;seem so glad&rdquo; and her &ldquo;anklets jingle so merrily,&rdquo; suggesting that Amal believes that the flower seller&rsquo;s daughter is cheerful. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the quotation makes no observation about the cheerful mood of the flower seller&rsquo;s daughter. Choice C is incorrect because the quotation discusses how Amal envisions his future, not the feelings of the flower seller&rsquo;s daughter. Choice D is incorrect because the quotation discusses Amal&rsquo;s wishes, not the feelings of the flower seller&rsquo;s daughter. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-402-LIT","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"3e4792a5-9658-42ee-84d7-228912f12c73","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959336,"updateDate":1691007959336}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959336, 1691007959336),
    ($SATQ$39e440e4$SATQ$, $SATQ$fd1571d8-4dd2-4b3e-acc1-33b0974356d2$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$51874be2-58af-4d3f-92ac-572d9f0bb0ee$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Archaeologists have held that the Casarabe culture, which emerged in the southwestern Amazon basin in the first millennium CE, was characterized by a sparse, widely distributed population and little intervention in the surrounding wilderness. Recently, however, archaeologist Heiko Pr&uuml;mers and colleagues conducted a study of the region using remote-sensing technology that enabled them to create three-dimensional images of the jungle-covered landscape from above, and the researchers concluded that the Casarabe people developed a form of urbanism in the Amazon basin.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which finding about the remote-sensing images, if true, would most directly support Pr&uuml;mers and colleagues&rsquo; conclusion?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"54156d18-ff7c-4de4-b37d-cd1363a38953","content":"<p>They show shapes consistent with widely separated settlements of roughly equal small size surrounded by uncultivated jungle.</p>"},{"id":"f63392fe-daa9-499b-8fc6-cbb4bd7aaa27","content":"<p>They show shapes consistent with long-distance footpaths running from Casarabe territories to large cities outside the region inhabited by the Casarabe people.</p>"},{"id":"54b19d29-0450-4806-886c-d2fbcb8337e7","content":"<p>&nbsp;They show shapes consistent with scattered small farms created by clearing jungle areas near sources of fresh water.</p>"},{"id":"8b834b4c-1bc3-4dd1-81e4-b38fbb224919","content":"<p>They show shapes consistent with monumental platforms and dense central settlements linked to smaller settlements by a system of canals and roadways.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["8b834b4c-1bc3-4dd1-81e4-b38fbb224919"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. This finding, if true, would support the archaeologists&rsquo; conclusion. Dense central settlements linked to smaller ones would provide evidence of cities and suburbs&mdash;in other words, &ldquo;a form of urbanism.&rdquo; </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This finding, if true, would weaken the archaeologists&rsquo; conclusion. Widely separated, small settlements with jungle in between would support the long-held belief that the Casarabe culture &ldquo;was characterized by a sparse, widely distributed population and little intervention in the surrounding wilderness.&rdquo; Choice B is incorrect. This choice wouldn&rsquo;t support the researchers&rsquo; conclusion. These large cities are located outside Casarabe territory, which doesn&rsquo;t show evidence of Casarabe urbanism. Choice C is incorrect. This finding wouldn&rsquo;t support the archaeologists&rsquo; conclusion. Scattered small farms in jungle clearings are not good evidence to support the existence of cities (&ldquo;a form of urbanism&rdquo;). </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TXT-H008$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$08722435-b811-4e82-9f49-e3d449f814e7$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"39e440e4","external_id":"fd1571d8-4dd2-4b3e-acc1-33b0974356d2","vaultid":"51874be2-58af-4d3f-92ac-572d9f0bb0ee","uId":"4a62e0c2-4f25-42c1-809c-619f70602032","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Archaeologists have held that the Casarabe culture, which emerged in the southwestern Amazon basin in the first millennium CE, was characterized by a sparse, widely distributed population and little intervention in the surrounding wilderness. Recently, however, archaeologist Heiko Pr&uuml;mers and colleagues conducted a study of the region using remote-sensing technology that enabled them to create three-dimensional images of the jungle-covered landscape from above, and the researchers concluded that the Casarabe people developed a form of urbanism in the Amazon basin.</p>","stem":"<p>Which finding about the remote-sensing images, if true, would most directly support Pr&uuml;mers and colleagues&rsquo; conclusion?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"54156d18-ff7c-4de4-b37d-cd1363a38953","content":"<p>They show shapes consistent with widely separated settlements of roughly equal small size surrounded by uncultivated jungle.</p>"},{"id":"f63392fe-daa9-499b-8fc6-cbb4bd7aaa27","content":"<p>They show shapes consistent with long-distance footpaths running from Casarabe territories to large cities outside the region inhabited by the Casarabe people.</p>"},{"id":"54b19d29-0450-4806-886c-d2fbcb8337e7","content":"<p>&nbsp;They show shapes consistent with scattered small farms created by clearing jungle areas near sources of fresh water.</p>"},{"id":"8b834b4c-1bc3-4dd1-81e4-b38fbb224919","content":"<p>They show shapes consistent with monumental platforms and dense central settlements linked to smaller settlements by a system of canals and roadways.</p>"}],"keys":["8b834b4c-1bc3-4dd1-81e4-b38fbb224919"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. This finding, if true, would support the archaeologists&rsquo; conclusion. Dense central settlements linked to smaller ones would provide evidence of cities and suburbs&mdash;in other words, &ldquo;a form of urbanism.&rdquo; </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This finding, if true, would weaken the archaeologists&rsquo; conclusion. Widely separated, small settlements with jungle in between would support the long-held belief that the Casarabe culture &ldquo;was characterized by a sparse, widely distributed population and little intervention in the surrounding wilderness.&rdquo; Choice B is incorrect. This choice wouldn&rsquo;t support the researchers&rsquo; conclusion. These large cities are located outside Casarabe territory, which doesn&rsquo;t show evidence of Casarabe urbanism. Choice C is incorrect. This finding wouldn&rsquo;t support the archaeologists&rsquo; conclusion. Scattered small farms in jungle clearings are not good evidence to support the existence of cities (&ldquo;a form of urbanism&rdquo;). </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TXT-H008","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"08722435-b811-4e82-9f49-e3d449f814e7","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959376,"updateDate":1691007959376}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959376, 1691007959376),
    ($SATQ$3a1f02b0$SATQ$, $SATQ$c0a0b3da-d759-43c9-8b98-ede924bab5da$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$5899abda-1951-4db1-8d2a-61c19917cbff$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from Frances Hodgson Burnett&rsquo;s 1911 novel <em>The Secret Garden</em>. Mary, a young girl, recently found an overgrown hidden garden.</p>
<p style='padding-left: 1em;'>Mary was an odd, determined little person, and now she had something interesting to be determined about, she was very much absorbed, indeed. She worked and dug and pulled up weeds steadily, only becoming more pleased with her work every hour instead of tiring of it. It seemed to her like a fascinating sort of play.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"020b2e3e-ba89-467c-a8aa-dd80fe9707a3","content":"<p>Mary hides in the garden to avoid doing her chores.</p>"},{"id":"b2deb843-d28f-4bb2-84d3-072d5634039d","content":"<p>Mary is getting bored with pulling up so many weeds in the garden.</p>"},{"id":"53bd8681-53cb-4890-b6d4-b4585c86c4c2","content":"<p>Mary is clearing out the garden to create a space to play.</p>"},{"id":"c56b72de-4587-4a02-ad13-72f92e33ebee","content":"<p>Mary feels very satisfied when she&rsquo;s taking care of the garden.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c56b72de-4587-4a02-ad13-72f92e33ebee"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most accurately states the main idea of the text. The text describes Mary&rsquo;s activities in an overgrown hidden garden, saying that she was &ldquo;very much absorbed&rdquo; and was &ldquo;only becoming more pleased with her work every hour&rdquo; rather than getting tired of it. She also thinks of garden activities as a &ldquo;fascinating sort of play.&rdquo; Thus, the main idea of the text is that Mary feels very satisfied when taking care of the garden. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text never makes any mention of Mary&rsquo;s chores. Choice B is incorrect because the text indicates that Mary finds pulling up weeds to be fascinating, not boring. Choice C is incorrect because Mary thinks of garden activities in and of themselves as play, not as something necessary to do to create a space to play. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-015-CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$66e44e5f-5d37-4ea7-8c7c-a9b37bf9613f$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"3a1f02b0","external_id":"c0a0b3da-d759-43c9-8b98-ede924bab5da","vaultid":"5899abda-1951-4db1-8d2a-61c19917cbff","uId":"b5b0cc68-7759-4c00-8692-cf353518733a","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from Frances Hodgson Burnett&rsquo;s 1911 novel <em>The Secret Garden</em>. Mary, a young girl, recently found an overgrown hidden garden.</p>\n<p style='padding-left: 1em;'>Mary was an odd, determined little person, and now she had something interesting to be determined about, she was very much absorbed, indeed. She worked and dug and pulled up weeds steadily, only becoming more pleased with her work every hour instead of tiring of it. It seemed to her like a fascinating sort of play.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"020b2e3e-ba89-467c-a8aa-dd80fe9707a3","content":"<p>Mary hides in the garden to avoid doing her chores.</p>"},{"id":"b2deb843-d28f-4bb2-84d3-072d5634039d","content":"<p>Mary is getting bored with pulling up so many weeds in the garden.</p>"},{"id":"53bd8681-53cb-4890-b6d4-b4585c86c4c2","content":"<p>Mary is clearing out the garden to create a space to play.</p>"},{"id":"c56b72de-4587-4a02-ad13-72f92e33ebee","content":"<p>Mary feels very satisfied when she&rsquo;s taking care of the garden.</p>"}],"keys":["c56b72de-4587-4a02-ad13-72f92e33ebee"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most accurately states the main idea of the text. The text describes Mary&rsquo;s activities in an overgrown hidden garden, saying that she was &ldquo;very much absorbed&rdquo; and was &ldquo;only becoming more pleased with her work every hour&rdquo; rather than getting tired of it. She also thinks of garden activities as a &ldquo;fascinating sort of play.&rdquo; Thus, the main idea of the text is that Mary feels very satisfied when taking care of the garden. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text never makes any mention of Mary&rsquo;s chores. Choice B is incorrect because the text indicates that Mary finds pulling up weeds to be fascinating, not boring. Choice C is incorrect because Mary thinks of garden activities in and of themselves as play, not as something necessary to do to create a space to play. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-015-CID","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"66e44e5f-5d37-4ea7-8c7c-a9b37bf9613f","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959315,"updateDate":1691007959315}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959315, 1691007959315),
    ($SATQ$3ae2638c$SATQ$, $SATQ$4903548d-ce23-40ab-b282-bcfd754299a0$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$95db0723-1b60-4fde-bb86-9ed43ba27691$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inferences$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In documents called judicial opinions, judges explain the reasoning behind their legal rulings, and in those explanations they sometimes cite and discuss historical and contemporary philosophers. Legal scholar and philosopher Anita L. Allen argues that while judges are naturally inclined to mention philosophers whose views align with their own positions, the strongest judicial opinions consider and rebut potential objections; discussing philosophers whose views conflict with judges&rsquo; views could therefore <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"c22c46a1-c080-4223-9a42-5e6821a2f0bd","content":"<p>allow judges to craft judicial opinions without needing to consult philosophical works.</p>"},{"id":"e5a276d0-690d-46ff-8e36-0f4520b1eda3","content":"<p>help judges improve the arguments they put forward in their judicial opinions.</p>"},{"id":"7d153ac8-e2cf-4ec6-a487-bfa58665dba8","content":"<p>make judicial opinions more comprehensible to readers without legal or philosophical training.</p>"},{"id":"5de042b4-a376-4fee-8add-d8393caa2ba0","content":"<p>bring judicial opinions in line with views that are broadly held among philosophers.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["e5a276d0-690d-46ff-8e36-0f4520b1eda3"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Anita Allen&rsquo;s argument about judges citing philosophers in their judicial opinions. The text indicates that judges sometimes cite philosophers when writing their judicial opinions and that, according to Allen, judges tend to cite philosophers whose views are in agreement with those of the judges themselves. Allen claims, however, that the best judicial opinions consider potential objections and rebut them, which suggests that judges may be able to strengthen their opinions by including discussions of philosophers with views contrary to their own.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because Allen&rsquo;s claim is that judges could improve their judicial opinions by citing philosophers who disagree with the views expressed in the opinions, which would necessarily require judges to consult philosophical works.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because there&rsquo;s no discussion in the text about making judicial opinions more easily understood by any particular group of readers. The focus of the text is on Allen&rsquo;s claim that judicial opinions could be strengthened by the inclusion of discussions of philosophers whose views disagree with those of the judges authoring the opinions.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because the text presents Allen&rsquo;s argument that discussing philosophers whose views judges disagree with could strengthen judicial opinions, not that doing so could bring those opinions into line with views that are popular among philosophers.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-013-INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$c684a3e6-1d0c-4915-ab63-9ef3a41260e5$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"3ae2638c","external_id":"4903548d-ce23-40ab-b282-bcfd754299a0","vaultid":"95db0723-1b60-4fde-bb86-9ed43ba27691","uId":"baf83067-7574-40cc-a7f8-76b613a3e0c7","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"INF","skill_desc":"Inferences","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In documents called judicial opinions, judges explain the reasoning behind their legal rulings, and in those explanations they sometimes cite and discuss historical and contemporary philosophers. Legal scholar and philosopher Anita L. Allen argues that while judges are naturally inclined to mention philosophers whose views align with their own positions, the strongest judicial opinions consider and rebut potential objections; discussing philosophers whose views conflict with judges&rsquo; views could therefore <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"c22c46a1-c080-4223-9a42-5e6821a2f0bd","content":"<p>allow judges to craft judicial opinions without needing to consult philosophical works.</p>"},{"id":"e5a276d0-690d-46ff-8e36-0f4520b1eda3","content":"<p>help judges improve the arguments they put forward in their judicial opinions.</p>"},{"id":"7d153ac8-e2cf-4ec6-a487-bfa58665dba8","content":"<p>make judicial opinions more comprehensible to readers without legal or philosophical training.</p>"},{"id":"5de042b4-a376-4fee-8add-d8393caa2ba0","content":"<p>bring judicial opinions in line with views that are broadly held among philosophers.</p>"}],"keys":["e5a276d0-690d-46ff-8e36-0f4520b1eda3"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Anita Allen&rsquo;s argument about judges citing philosophers in their judicial opinions. The text indicates that judges sometimes cite philosophers when writing their judicial opinions and that, according to Allen, judges tend to cite philosophers whose views are in agreement with those of the judges themselves. Allen claims, however, that the best judicial opinions consider potential objections and rebut them, which suggests that judges may be able to strengthen their opinions by including discussions of philosophers with views contrary to their own.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because Allen&rsquo;s claim is that judges could improve their judicial opinions by citing philosophers who disagree with the views expressed in the opinions, which would necessarily require judges to consult philosophical works.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because there&rsquo;s no discussion in the text about making judicial opinions more easily understood by any particular group of readers. The focus of the text is on Allen&rsquo;s claim that judicial opinions could be strengthened by the inclusion of discussions of philosophers whose views disagree with those of the judges authoring the opinions.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because the text presents Allen&rsquo;s argument that discussing philosophers whose views judges disagree with could strengthen judicial opinions, not that doing so could bring those opinions into line with views that are popular among philosophers.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-013-INF","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"c684a3e6-1d0c-4915-ab63-9ef3a41260e5","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959315,"updateDate":1691007959315}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959315, 1691007959315),
    ($SATQ$3bfcb73b$SATQ$, $SATQ$79e4ee43-9828-46ac-afcb-d3678e7e0d1c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$50d792dc-f4e8-421b-b9c7-1bed55c0071f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>An archaeological team led by Piotr Bieliński and Sultan al-Bakri found remnants of a 4,000-year-old Bronze Age board game at a site in Oman. Little is left of the game except a stone board, which is carved with a grid and has places to hold game pieces. Some scholars claim that the game was largely played by traders.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which finding, if true, would most directly support the scholars&rsquo; claim?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"3cf96b6b-6959-413f-ab7e-113b3042a1ec","content":"<p>Other examples of the game dating to the same period have been found in the remains of several homes in the region, including in one home that may have belonged to a trader.</p>"},{"id":"1c257f8c-7b0f-4bbb-ba1c-91a0e3050bbb","content":"<p>Similar games have been found in other sites dating to the same period that were connected to the site in Oman via trade routes.</p>"},{"id":"688ebd35-ff37-4b02-9cc0-23d3714334c4","content":"<p>The other known examples of the game dating to the same period have been found along routes that seem to have been used primarily by traders at the time.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"50693ec8-c555-436f-8f02-7268e71e9cab","content":"<p>Remnants of other goods have been found at the site in Oman that probably also reached the location through trade.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["688ebd35-ff37-4b02-9cc0-23d3714334c4"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it presents a finding that, if true, would most directly support the scholars&rsquo; claim about the board game. The text explains that the remains of a 4,000-year-old board game were found in Oman. The text then states that scholars claim this board game was played mostly by traders. If the other known examples of this board game from the same time period were discovered along routes that seem to have been used primarily by traders, this finding would directly support the scholars&rsquo; claim because it suggests that the game was largely played by traders who brought it with them for entertainment as they traveled. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because this finding would suggest only that a single trader may have possessed examples of the board game, perhaps for the purpose of trading or selling the game to residents of Oman. For this reason, the finding wouldn&rsquo;t directly support the scholars&rsquo; claim that the majority of the game&rsquo;s players were traders. Choice B is incorrect because this finding doesn&rsquo;t mention the board game at all, referring only to similar games found at other sites, and would therefore provide no direct support for the scholars&rsquo; claim about the board game. Choice D is incorrect because this finding doesn&rsquo;t mention the board game at all, referring only to the remains of other goods found at the site in Oman, and would therefore provide no direct support for the scholars&rsquo; claim about the board game. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-333-TXT$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$e2fe4399-c965-4c86-8f60-3c38e78622c2$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"3bfcb73b","external_id":"79e4ee43-9828-46ac-afcb-d3678e7e0d1c","vaultid":"50d792dc-f4e8-421b-b9c7-1bed55c0071f","uId":"59b1c708-0475-48cb-a5b8-00df975345c8","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>An archaeological team led by Piotr Bieliński and Sultan al-Bakri found remnants of a 4,000-year-old Bronze Age board game at a site in Oman. Little is left of the game except a stone board, which is carved with a grid and has places to hold game pieces. Some scholars claim that the game was largely played by traders.</p>","stem":"<p>Which finding, if true, would most directly support the scholars&rsquo; claim?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"3cf96b6b-6959-413f-ab7e-113b3042a1ec","content":"<p>Other examples of the game dating to the same period have been found in the remains of several homes in the region, including in one home that may have belonged to a trader.</p>"},{"id":"1c257f8c-7b0f-4bbb-ba1c-91a0e3050bbb","content":"<p>Similar games have been found in other sites dating to the same period that were connected to the site in Oman via trade routes.</p>"},{"id":"688ebd35-ff37-4b02-9cc0-23d3714334c4","content":"<p>The other known examples of the game dating to the same period have been found along routes that seem to have been used primarily by traders at the time.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"50693ec8-c555-436f-8f02-7268e71e9cab","content":"<p>Remnants of other goods have been found at the site in Oman that probably also reached the location through trade.</p>"}],"keys":["688ebd35-ff37-4b02-9cc0-23d3714334c4"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it presents a finding that, if true, would most directly support the scholars&rsquo; claim about the board game. The text explains that the remains of a 4,000-year-old board game were found in Oman. The text then states that scholars claim this board game was played mostly by traders. If the other known examples of this board game from the same time period were discovered along routes that seem to have been used primarily by traders, this finding would directly support the scholars&rsquo; claim because it suggests that the game was largely played by traders who brought it with them for entertainment as they traveled. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because this finding would suggest only that a single trader may have possessed examples of the board game, perhaps for the purpose of trading or selling the game to residents of Oman. For this reason, the finding wouldn&rsquo;t directly support the scholars&rsquo; claim that the majority of the game&rsquo;s players were traders. Choice B is incorrect because this finding doesn&rsquo;t mention the board game at all, referring only to similar games found at other sites, and would therefore provide no direct support for the scholars&rsquo; claim about the board game. Choice D is incorrect because this finding doesn&rsquo;t mention the board game at all, referring only to the remains of other goods found at the site in Oman, and would therefore provide no direct support for the scholars&rsquo; claim about the board game. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-333-TXT","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"e2fe4399-c965-4c86-8f60-3c38e78622c2","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959336,"updateDate":1691007959336}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959336, 1691007959336),
    ($SATQ$3cc2eacc$SATQ$, $SATQ$03bfc4d5-8347-47d5-b316-718229d88af8$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$3398ffaf-f635-4df2-8d30-0a44799277a5$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inferences$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In a study of the mechanisms underlying associative memory&mdash;or the ability to learn and remember connections between inherently unrelated things&mdash;neuroscientists Kei Igarashi, Jasmine Chavez, and others presented mice with memory tests. The team discovered that fan cells, a type of cell found in the medial temporal lobe of the brain, are necessary for the acquisition of new associative memories. They also found that fan cell activity requires dopamine, a chemical the brain produces in response to pleasure and rewards. Consequently, receiving a reward should likely help to <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"2b40a659-cac5-4f0b-ab7f-4ce8bf5910e7","content":"<p>decrease an individual&rsquo;s capacity to utilize dopamine.</p>"},{"id":"0ed2c810-d394-4632-9f4b-3313940a3cd9","content":"<p>increase an individual&rsquo;s capacity to recognize differences between unrelated things.</p>"},{"id":"ff3ac0cb-e607-47a5-86ad-b43f96244b1e","content":"<p>increase an individual&rsquo;s capacity to form associative memories.</p>"},{"id":"7af1fd04-4f44-4973-a4f8-cf937dc7bda2","content":"<p>decrease an individual&rsquo;s capacity to create fan cells.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ff3ac0cb-e607-47a5-86ad-b43f96244b1e"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the mechanisms underlying associative memory. The text explains that fan cells&mdash;a type of brain cell&mdash;are necessary for the acquisition of new associative memories, and that activity among these cells requires a chemical known as dopamine, which the brain produces in response to rewards. Since the brain cells that enable the formation of associative memories require dopamine in order to function, and since the brain produces dopamine in response to rewards, it can be inferred that receiving a reward should likely help to increase an individual&rsquo;s capacity to form associative memories. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the relationship between rewards and dopamine sketched by the text is that rewards result in the production of dopamine, not that they cause an individual&rsquo;s capacity to utilize dopamine to decrease. Choice B is incorrect. The text suggests that receiving a reward would produce dopamine and thereby assist with associative memory formation. However, the text never suggests that associative memory involves the capacity to recognize differences between unrelated things, indicating only that associative memory involves remembering what connects those things. Choice D is incorrect because the text never discusses how fan cells are initially created and therefore provides no evidence for a conclusion about how receiving a reward would affect their creation.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-022-INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$114fa07a-8f2a-4bd0-b7cc-65bc65243c9a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"3cc2eacc","external_id":"03bfc4d5-8347-47d5-b316-718229d88af8","vaultid":"3398ffaf-f635-4df2-8d30-0a44799277a5","uId":"4713b775-a24c-42e4-8c5c-3cffc01db68e","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"INF","skill_desc":"Inferences","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In a study of the mechanisms underlying associative memory&mdash;or the ability to learn and remember connections between inherently unrelated things&mdash;neuroscientists Kei Igarashi, Jasmine Chavez, and others presented mice with memory tests. The team discovered that fan cells, a type of cell found in the medial temporal lobe of the brain, are necessary for the acquisition of new associative memories. They also found that fan cell activity requires dopamine, a chemical the brain produces in response to pleasure and rewards. Consequently, receiving a reward should likely help to <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"2b40a659-cac5-4f0b-ab7f-4ce8bf5910e7","content":"<p>decrease an individual&rsquo;s capacity to utilize dopamine.</p>"},{"id":"0ed2c810-d394-4632-9f4b-3313940a3cd9","content":"<p>increase an individual&rsquo;s capacity to recognize differences between unrelated things.</p>"},{"id":"ff3ac0cb-e607-47a5-86ad-b43f96244b1e","content":"<p>increase an individual&rsquo;s capacity to form associative memories.</p>"},{"id":"7af1fd04-4f44-4973-a4f8-cf937dc7bda2","content":"<p>decrease an individual&rsquo;s capacity to create fan cells.</p>"}],"keys":["ff3ac0cb-e607-47a5-86ad-b43f96244b1e"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the mechanisms underlying associative memory. The text explains that fan cells&mdash;a type of brain cell&mdash;are necessary for the acquisition of new associative memories, and that activity among these cells requires a chemical known as dopamine, which the brain produces in response to rewards. Since the brain cells that enable the formation of associative memories require dopamine in order to function, and since the brain produces dopamine in response to rewards, it can be inferred that receiving a reward should likely help to increase an individual&rsquo;s capacity to form associative memories. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the relationship between rewards and dopamine sketched by the text is that rewards result in the production of dopamine, not that they cause an individual&rsquo;s capacity to utilize dopamine to decrease. Choice B is incorrect. The text suggests that receiving a reward would produce dopamine and thereby assist with associative memory formation. However, the text never suggests that associative memory involves the capacity to recognize differences between unrelated things, indicating only that associative memory involves remembering what connects those things. Choice D is incorrect because the text never discusses how fan cells are initially created and therefore provides no evidence for a conclusion about how receiving a reward would affect their creation.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-022-INF","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"114fa07a-8f2a-4bd0-b7cc-65bc65243c9a","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959317,"updateDate":1691007959317}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959317, 1691007959317),
    ($SATQ$3cc7d73b$SATQ$, $SATQ$7fac536f-bfa4-41d3-b7ea-1adb6680cd47$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Using the Stratospheric Observatory for Infrared Astronomy (SOFIA), a team of astronomers mapped out the magnetic field of G47, one of the Milky Way&rsquo;s galactic bones (dense clouds of gas and dust that run through the middle of the arm of a spiral galaxy). Surprisingly, the map revealed a magnetic field with no clear pattern or direction. The researchers had expected the magnetic field to be similar to the more uniform fields seen in galactic bones in other arms of the Milky Way.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>According to the text, what was surprising about the researchers&rsquo; mapping of the magnetic field of galactic bone G47?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4150f4ca-f20c-4815-8166-a999c830a457","content":"<p>It showed a weaker magnetic field than expected.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"b32c3dc6-bebd-4988-91a5-ad052eaafae6","content":"<p>It implied that previous mappings of the magnetic field were inaccurate.</p>"},{"id":"b6589340-c486-4c95-b5ef-02f15d55047e","content":"<p>It produced magnetic field measurements similar to those for other galactic bones.</p>"},{"id":"5fdf7108-a375-44f9-aac6-e14241505fd7","content":"<p>It revealed a magnetic field that wasn&rsquo;t uniform.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["5fdf7108-a375-44f9-aac6-e14241505fd7"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it presents the point about the magnetic field mapping that the text describes as surprising. The text indicates that a team of astronomers mapped the magnetic field of G47, a galactic bone in the Milky Way, and that the mapping "surprisingly" revealed "no clear pattern or direction" in the magnetic field. The text then adds that the researchers had thought the magnetic field would be as uniform as the magnetic fields of other galactic bones in the Milky Way are. In other words, the researchers were surprised that the mapping revealed a magnetic field that wasn&rsquo;t uniform.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t indicate that the mapping surprisingly showed a weaker magnetic field than the researchers had expected. The text makes no mention of the magnetic field&rsquo;s strength, only its lack of a clear pattern or direction. Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t indicate that the magnetic field mapping suggested to researchers that previous mappings were inaccurate. Although the text states that the mapping didn&rsquo;t show what researchers had expected based on their knowledge of other magnetic fields, there&rsquo;s no indication that G47&rsquo;s magnetic field had ever been mapped before. Choice C is incorrect because the text indicates that the researchers were surprised that the magnetic field was different from, not similar to, the magnetic fields of other galactic bones; the text states that because other galactic bones in the Milky Way are more uniform, the researchers had expected the magnetic field of G47 to be more uniform than it turned out to be.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"3cc7d73b","external_id":"7fac536f-bfa4-41d3-b7ea-1adb6680cd47","vaultid":null,"uId":"89bd338e-a9fb-4936-8a4b-d14fa487287c","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Using the Stratospheric Observatory for Infrared Astronomy (SOFIA), a team of astronomers mapped out the magnetic field of G47, one of the Milky Way&rsquo;s galactic bones (dense clouds of gas and dust that run through the middle of the arm of a spiral galaxy). Surprisingly, the map revealed a magnetic field with no clear pattern or direction. The researchers had expected the magnetic field to be similar to the more uniform fields seen in galactic bones in other arms of the Milky Way.</p>","stem":"<p>According to the text, what was surprising about the researchers&rsquo; mapping of the magnetic field of galactic bone G47?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4150f4ca-f20c-4815-8166-a999c830a457","content":"<p>It showed a weaker magnetic field than expected.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"b32c3dc6-bebd-4988-91a5-ad052eaafae6","content":"<p>It implied that previous mappings of the magnetic field were inaccurate.</p>"},{"id":"b6589340-c486-4c95-b5ef-02f15d55047e","content":"<p>It produced magnetic field measurements similar to those for other galactic bones.</p>"},{"id":"5fdf7108-a375-44f9-aac6-e14241505fd7","content":"<p>It revealed a magnetic field that wasn&rsquo;t uniform.</p>"}],"keys":["5fdf7108-a375-44f9-aac6-e14241505fd7"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it presents the point about the magnetic field mapping that the text describes as surprising. The text indicates that a team of astronomers mapped the magnetic field of G47, a galactic bone in the Milky Way, and that the mapping \"surprisingly\" revealed \"no clear pattern or direction\" in the magnetic field. The text then adds that the researchers had thought the magnetic field would be as uniform as the magnetic fields of other galactic bones in the Milky Way are. In other words, the researchers were surprised that the mapping revealed a magnetic field that wasn&rsquo;t uniform.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t indicate that the mapping surprisingly showed a weaker magnetic field than the researchers had expected. The text makes no mention of the magnetic field&rsquo;s strength, only its lack of a clear pattern or direction. Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t indicate that the magnetic field mapping suggested to researchers that previous mappings were inaccurate. Although the text states that the mapping didn&rsquo;t show what researchers had expected based on their knowledge of other magnetic fields, there&rsquo;s no indication that G47&rsquo;s magnetic field had ever been mapped before. Choice C is incorrect because the text indicates that the researchers were surprised that the magnetic field was different from, not similar to, the magnetic fields of other galactic bones; the text states that because other galactic bones in the Milky Way are more uniform, the researchers had expected the magnetic field of G47 to be more uniform than it turned out to be.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323040,"updateDate":1730147323040}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323040, 1730147323040),
    ($SATQ$3d505895$SATQ$, $SATQ$ca85c18e-96d2-4d6d-bca4-d94ece969e9a$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inferences$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Parthenogenesis is a form of reproduction in which a female reproduces without mating. She produces an egg containing a single offspring whose DNA, or genetic material, comes entirely from its mother. Among birds, parthenogenesis has been found in a handful of species, including pigeons and turkeys. When scientists at the San Diego Zoo analyzed the DNA of the zoo&rsquo;s California condors (a species of vulture), they discovered that two individuals weren&rsquo;t genetically similar enough to any of the males in the condor enclosure to be their offspring. However, both had hatched from eggs laid by females in the enclosure. Thus, the scientists concluded that <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"8bd80e7b-3bc6-4ea3-a1d8-a32f053cefef","content":"<p>at least one of those individuals&rsquo; mothers was born as a result of parthenogenesis.</p>"},{"id":"e3599585-b983-4dc7-87cb-c7fa0d4590ba","content":"<p>California condors may reproduce through parthenogenesis in zoos but not in the wild.</p>"},{"id":"7844e3cc-b317-411a-82d3-8af7e10b0391","content":"<p>the mothers of the two individuals probably reproduced through parthenogenesis.</p>"},{"id":"c0835442-d638-4465-9196-7ce12963096d","content":"<p>California condors reproduce through parthenogenesis only if females lack sufficient access to males.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["7844e3cc-b317-411a-82d3-8af7e10b0391"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of parthenogenesis as it relates to two California condors at the San Diego Zoo. The text explains that parthenogenesis is a form of reproduction in which a female reproduces without mating, producing an egg containing an offspring with DNA that comes entirely from its mother. After noting examples of parthenogenesis among birds, the text then reports on a discovery at the San Diego Zoo. According to the text, scientists at the zoo found that two of the zoo&rsquo;s California condors weren&rsquo;t similar enough to any of the zoo&rsquo;s male condors to be their offspring. Given that parthenogenesis occurs without mating and results in an offspring with DNA entirely from its mother, it can reasonably be inferred that the scientists concluded that the mothers of the two condors probably didn&rsquo;t mate with any of the male condors at the zoo, reproducing through parthenogenesis instead.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text never suggests that at least one of the condors&rsquo; mothers was born as a result of parthenogenesis. Rather, the text suggests that the two condors were themselves both probably born as a result of parthenogenesis, which would account for their lack of strong genetic resemblance to any of the male condors in the zoo&rsquo;s condor enclosure. Choice B is incorrect because the text never discusses wild California condors, only California condors living at the San Diego Zoo. For this reason, the text doesn&rsquo;t support a conclusion that distinguishes between how California condors reproduce in zoos and how they reproduce in the wild. Choice D is incorrect because the text never suggests that the zoo&rsquo;s female condors lacked sufficient access to males or that this would be a cause of parthenogenesis, indicating only that the condor enclosure included males.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"3d505895","external_id":"ca85c18e-96d2-4d6d-bca4-d94ece969e9a","vaultid":null,"uId":"9a848504-e505-41a1-89c4-88c1ac4d3c90","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"INF","skill_desc":"Inferences","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Parthenogenesis is a form of reproduction in which a female reproduces without mating. She produces an egg containing a single offspring whose DNA, or genetic material, comes entirely from its mother. Among birds, parthenogenesis has been found in a handful of species, including pigeons and turkeys. When scientists at the San Diego Zoo analyzed the DNA of the zoo&rsquo;s California condors (a species of vulture), they discovered that two individuals weren&rsquo;t genetically similar enough to any of the males in the condor enclosure to be their offspring. However, both had hatched from eggs laid by females in the enclosure. Thus, the scientists concluded that <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"8bd80e7b-3bc6-4ea3-a1d8-a32f053cefef","content":"<p>at least one of those individuals&rsquo; mothers was born as a result of parthenogenesis.</p>"},{"id":"e3599585-b983-4dc7-87cb-c7fa0d4590ba","content":"<p>California condors may reproduce through parthenogenesis in zoos but not in the wild.</p>"},{"id":"7844e3cc-b317-411a-82d3-8af7e10b0391","content":"<p>the mothers of the two individuals probably reproduced through parthenogenesis.</p>"},{"id":"c0835442-d638-4465-9196-7ce12963096d","content":"<p>California condors reproduce through parthenogenesis only if females lack sufficient access to males.</p>"}],"keys":["7844e3cc-b317-411a-82d3-8af7e10b0391"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of parthenogenesis as it relates to two California condors at the San Diego Zoo. The text explains that parthenogenesis is a form of reproduction in which a female reproduces without mating, producing an egg containing an offspring with DNA that comes entirely from its mother. After noting examples of parthenogenesis among birds, the text then reports on a discovery at the San Diego Zoo. According to the text, scientists at the zoo found that two of the zoo&rsquo;s California condors weren&rsquo;t similar enough to any of the zoo&rsquo;s male condors to be their offspring. Given that parthenogenesis occurs without mating and results in an offspring with DNA entirely from its mother, it can reasonably be inferred that the scientists concluded that the mothers of the two condors probably didn&rsquo;t mate with any of the male condors at the zoo, reproducing through parthenogenesis instead.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text never suggests that at least one of the condors&rsquo; mothers was born as a result of parthenogenesis. Rather, the text suggests that the two condors were themselves both probably born as a result of parthenogenesis, which would account for their lack of strong genetic resemblance to any of the male condors in the zoo&rsquo;s condor enclosure. Choice B is incorrect because the text never discusses wild California condors, only California condors living at the San Diego Zoo. For this reason, the text doesn&rsquo;t support a conclusion that distinguishes between how California condors reproduce in zoos and how they reproduce in the wild. Choice D is incorrect because the text never suggests that the zoo&rsquo;s female condors lacked sufficient access to males or that this would be a cause of parthenogenesis, indicating only that the condor enclosure included males.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323035,"updateDate":1730147323035}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323035, 1730147323035),
    ($SATQ$3d91c973$SATQ$, $SATQ$f445072c-007e-485c-a469-b2b4bee641b3$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$5568d3fc-cafd-4223-948f-df6f11e51500$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Mosasaurs were large marine reptiles that lived in the Late Cretaceous period, approximately 100 million to 66 million years ago. Celina Suarez, Alberto P&eacute;rez-Huerta, and T. Lynn Harrell Jr. examined oxygen-18 isotopes in mosasaur tooth enamel in order to calculate likely mosasaur body temperatures and determined that mosasaurs were endothermic&mdash;that is, they used internal metabolic processes to maintain a stable body temperature in a variety of ambient temperatures. Suarez, P&eacute;rez-Huerta, and Harrell claim that endothermy would have enabled mosasaurs to include relatively cold polar waters in their range.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which finding, if true, would most directly support Suarez, P&eacute;rez-Huerta, and Harrell&rsquo;s claim?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"7131ae0e-67cf-4bf7-8e11-e3b11f8d8108","content":"<p>Mosasaurs&rsquo; likely body temperatures are easier to determine from tooth enamel oxygen-18 isotope data than the body temperatures of nonendothermic Late Cretaceous marine reptiles are.</p>"},{"id":"ef14ca93-169a-4a44-bf1a-d1a85b1596d0","content":"<p>Fossils of both mosasaurs and nonendothermic marine reptiles have been found in roughly equal numbers in regions known to be near the poles during the Late Cretaceous, though in lower concentrations than elsewhere.</p>"},{"id":"c79b3f8a-9989-472c-bc2b-95a5b7eb2617","content":"<p>Several mosasaur fossils have been found in regions known to be near the poles during the Late Cretaceous, while relatively few fossils of nonendothermic marine reptiles have been found in those locations.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"1bef58db-8fc0-42ec-b481-2439dcbba94e","content":"<p>During the Late Cretaceous, seawater temperatures were likely higher throughout mosasaurs&rsquo; range, including near the poles, than seawater temperatures at those same latitudes are today.&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c79b3f8a-9989-472c-bc2b-95a5b7eb2617"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it presents the finding that, if true, would best support Suarez, P&eacute;rez-Huerta, and Harrell&rsquo;s claim about mosasaurs. The text states that Suarez, P&eacute;rez-Huerta, and Harrell&rsquo;s research on mosasaur tooth enamel led them to conclude that mosasaurs were endothermic, which means that they could live in waters at many different temperatures and still maintain a stable body temperature. The researchers claim that endothermy enabled mosasaurs to live in relatively cold waters near the poles. If several mosasaur fossils have been found in areas that were near the poles during the period when mosasaurs were alive and fossils of nonendothermic marine reptiles are rare in such locations, that would support the researchers&rsquo; claim: it would show that mosasaurs inhabited polar waters but nonendothermic marine mammals tended not to, suggesting that endothermy may have been the characteristic that enabled mosasaurs to include polar waters in their range. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because finding that it&rsquo;s easier to determine mosasaur body temperatures from tooth enamel data than it is to determine nonendothermic reptile body temperatures wouldn&rsquo;t support the researchers&rsquo; claim. Whether one research process is more difficult than another indicates nothing about the results of those processes and therefore is irrelevant to the issue of where mosasaurs lived and what enabled them to live in those locations.&nbsp;
&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect because finding roughly equal numbers of mosasaur and nonendothermic marine reptile fossils in areas that were near the poles in the Late Cretaceous would suggest that endothermy didn&rsquo;t give mosasaurs any particular advantage when it came to expanding their range to include relatively cold polar waters, thereby weakening the researchers&rsquo; claim rather than supporting it. Choice D is incorrect because finding that the temperature of seawater in the Late Cretaceous was warmer than seawater today wouldn&rsquo;t weaken the researchers&rsquo; claim. Seawater in the Late Cretaceous could have been warmer than seawater today but still cold enough for endothermy to be advantageous to mosasaurs, so this finding wouldn&rsquo;t provide enough information to either support or weaken the researchers&rsquo; claim. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-311-TXT$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$babb6095-3b3c-4dda-986e-620490c204d1$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"3d91c973","external_id":"f445072c-007e-485c-a469-b2b4bee641b3","vaultid":"5568d3fc-cafd-4223-948f-df6f11e51500","uId":"1299a1b8-11f3-457d-a6e1-0b81596c5a1f","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Mosasaurs were large marine reptiles that lived in the Late Cretaceous period, approximately 100 million to 66 million years ago. Celina Suarez, Alberto P&eacute;rez-Huerta, and T. Lynn Harrell Jr. examined oxygen-18 isotopes in mosasaur tooth enamel in order to calculate likely mosasaur body temperatures and determined that mosasaurs were endothermic&mdash;that is, they used internal metabolic processes to maintain a stable body temperature in a variety of ambient temperatures. Suarez, P&eacute;rez-Huerta, and Harrell claim that endothermy would have enabled mosasaurs to include relatively cold polar waters in their range.</p>","stem":"<p>Which finding, if true, would most directly support Suarez, P&eacute;rez-Huerta, and Harrell&rsquo;s claim?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"7131ae0e-67cf-4bf7-8e11-e3b11f8d8108","content":"<p>Mosasaurs&rsquo; likely body temperatures are easier to determine from tooth enamel oxygen-18 isotope data than the body temperatures of nonendothermic Late Cretaceous marine reptiles are.</p>"},{"id":"ef14ca93-169a-4a44-bf1a-d1a85b1596d0","content":"<p>Fossils of both mosasaurs and nonendothermic marine reptiles have been found in roughly equal numbers in regions known to be near the poles during the Late Cretaceous, though in lower concentrations than elsewhere.</p>"},{"id":"c79b3f8a-9989-472c-bc2b-95a5b7eb2617","content":"<p>Several mosasaur fossils have been found in regions known to be near the poles during the Late Cretaceous, while relatively few fossils of nonendothermic marine reptiles have been found in those locations.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"1bef58db-8fc0-42ec-b481-2439dcbba94e","content":"<p>During the Late Cretaceous, seawater temperatures were likely higher throughout mosasaurs&rsquo; range, including near the poles, than seawater temperatures at those same latitudes are today.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["c79b3f8a-9989-472c-bc2b-95a5b7eb2617"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it presents the finding that, if true, would best support Suarez, P&eacute;rez-Huerta, and Harrell&rsquo;s claim about mosasaurs. The text states that Suarez, P&eacute;rez-Huerta, and Harrell&rsquo;s research on mosasaur tooth enamel led them to conclude that mosasaurs were endothermic, which means that they could live in waters at many different temperatures and still maintain a stable body temperature. The researchers claim that endothermy enabled mosasaurs to live in relatively cold waters near the poles. If several mosasaur fossils have been found in areas that were near the poles during the period when mosasaurs were alive and fossils of nonendothermic marine reptiles are rare in such locations, that would support the researchers&rsquo; claim: it would show that mosasaurs inhabited polar waters but nonendothermic marine mammals tended not to, suggesting that endothermy may have been the characteristic that enabled mosasaurs to include polar waters in their range. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because finding that it&rsquo;s easier to determine mosasaur body temperatures from tooth enamel data than it is to determine nonendothermic reptile body temperatures wouldn&rsquo;t support the researchers&rsquo; claim. Whether one research process is more difficult than another indicates nothing about the results of those processes and therefore is irrelevant to the issue of where mosasaurs lived and what enabled them to live in those locations.&nbsp;\n&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect because finding roughly equal numbers of mosasaur and nonendothermic marine reptile fossils in areas that were near the poles in the Late Cretaceous would suggest that endothermy didn&rsquo;t give mosasaurs any particular advantage when it came to expanding their range to include relatively cold polar waters, thereby weakening the researchers&rsquo; claim rather than supporting it. Choice D is incorrect because finding that the temperature of seawater in the Late Cretaceous was warmer than seawater today wouldn&rsquo;t weaken the researchers&rsquo; claim. Seawater in the Late Cretaceous could have been warmer than seawater today but still cold enough for endothermy to be advantageous to mosasaurs, so this finding wouldn&rsquo;t provide enough information to either support or weaken the researchers&rsquo; claim. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-311-TXT","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"babb6095-3b3c-4dda-986e-620490c204d1","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959335,"updateDate":1691007959335}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959335, 1691007959335),
